    "['ip access-list extended [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set firewall family inet filter test term 1 from source-address 10.10.1.1', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set firewall family inet filter test term 1 from source-address 10.10.1.1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset firewall family inet filter test term 1 from source-address 10.10.1.1\\nset firewall family inet filter test term 1 then discard\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- Juniper uses `firewall` instead of `access-list`\\n- `family inet` specifies IPv4 filtering (equivalent to Cisco\\'s `ip` in `ip access-list`)\\n- `filter test` creates a filter named \"test\" (like the ACL name)\\n- `term 1` defines the first matching condition (terms are roughly equivalent to ACL lines)\\n- The `from` clause matches the source address (`host 10.10.1.1` becomes `source-address 10.10.1.1`)\\n- The `then discard` action matches Cisco\\'s `deny` action\\n- Unlike Cisco\\'s implicit deny, Juniper filters require explicit terms for both allowed and denied traffic', 'trans_template': 'set firewall family inet filter test term 1 from source-address 10.10.1.1'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set firewall family inet filter test term 1 then discard', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set firewall family inet filter test term 1 then discard', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset firewall family inet filter test term 1 from source-address 10.10.1.1\\nset firewall family inet filter test term 1 then discard\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- Juniper uses `firewall` instead of `access-list`\\n- `family inet` specifies IPv4 filtering (equivalent to Cisco\\'s `ip` in `ip access-list`)\\n- `filter test` creates a filter named \"test\" (like the ACL name)\\n- `term 1` defines the first matching condition (terms are roughly equivalent to ACL lines)\\n- The `from` clause matches the source address (`host 10.10.1.1` becomes `source-address 10.10.1.1`)\\n- The `then discard` action matches Cisco\\'s `deny` action\\n- Unlike Cisco\\'s implicit deny, Juniper filters require explicit terms for both allowed and denied traffic', 'trans_template': 'set firewall family inet filter test term 1 then discard'}]]": 1,
    "['deny ip host [parameter1] any', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set firewall family inet filter test term deny_host from source-address 10.10.1.1/32', 'parent_command': ['set firewall family inet filter test term 1 from source-address 10.10.1.1'], 'root': 'set firewall family inet filter test term 1 from source-address 10.10.1.1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Juniper equivalent command for the Cisco ACL entry:\\n\\n##\\nset firewall family inet filter test term deny_host from source-address 10.10.1.1/32\\nset firewall family inet filter test term deny_host then discard\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. Juniper uses firewall filters instead of access lists\\n2. \"family inet\" specifies IPv4 processing\\n3. \"term deny_host\" creates a matching term (similar to a rule)\\n4. The source address is specified with /32 precision (equivalent to \"host\")\\n5. \"then discard\" is Juniper\\'s equivalent to \"deny\" in Cisco\\n6. Note this is part of a filter configuration that would need additional context to match the complete Cisco ACL application', 'trans_template': 'set firewall family inet filter test term deny_host from source-address 10.10.1.1/32'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set firewall family inet filter test term deny_host then discard', 'parent_command': ['set firewall family inet filter test term 1 from source-address 10.10.1.1'], 'root': 'set firewall family inet filter test term 1 from source-address 10.10.1.1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Juniper equivalent command for the Cisco ACL entry:\\n\\n##\\nset firewall family inet filter test term deny_host from source-address 10.10.1.1/32\\nset firewall family inet filter test term deny_host then discard\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. Juniper uses firewall filters instead of access lists\\n2. \"family inet\" specifies IPv4 processing\\n3. \"term deny_host\" creates a matching term (similar to a rule)\\n4. The source address is specified with /32 precision (equivalent to \"host\")\\n5. \"then discard\" is Juniper\\'s equivalent to \"deny\" in Cisco\\n6. Note this is part of a filter configuration that would need additional context to match the complete Cisco ACL application', 'trans_template': 'set firewall family inet filter test term deny_host then discard'}]]": 1,
